y, 1995–99, 🟥 10-yr Treasury, 2022–26, 🟩 Fed funds, 1995–99, 🟧 Fed funds, 2022–26. Source: https://fred.stlouisfed.org/graph/fredgraph.csv?id=GS10, https://fred.stlouisfed.org/graph/fredgraph.csv?id=FEDFUNDS\n\n- **Then:** in 1999 the 10-year yield rose from 4.7% to 6.3%, and the Fed started raising rates in Jun 1999, near the end of the run.\n- **Now:** the 10-year is up from 4.14% (Dec 2025) to **5.13%** (Oct 2026). The Fed's policy rate has turned up from 3.63% to 3.88% since August, at almost the same point on the clock.\n\n### 5. What doesn't match\n- **Momentum:** the S&P's trailing 5-year return is **10.6% a year**, against **24.3%** at the Jan 2000 peak. The trailing 3-year return is 18.3%, against 26.5%. The 5-year figure still includes the 2022 bear market. The full report has this chart.\n- **No blow-off yet:** in the last seven months of 1999–2000 the Nasdaq rose 67%, and the Nasdaq/S&P ratio jumped 47%. Nothing like that has happened this time.\n- **Tech is already lagging:** the Nasdaq peaked in May 2026 and is 6.8% below that high. The S&P is 1.2% below its September high.\n\n**Bottom line:** valuations and rates look like 1999, but the size and speed of the run look more like 1997–98. What would make the comparison more convincing is a Nasdaq blow-off, or the 10-year yield staying above 5% while the Fed keeps raising rates. Even then, the two ways of lining up the charts are about 18 months apart on where we are, so the comparison can't time a top.\n\n<sub>The S&P 500 and Nasdaq monthly closes come from Yahoo Finance, because Financial Datasets and Equibles price history only goes back to 2020–2023. Other sources: CAPE from multpl.com (Shiller data), rates from FRED, and P/E ratios from api.financialdatasets.ai. These are price returns only, without dividends, and November 2026 is month-to-date. None of this is investment advice.</sub>"}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"nvidia","model":"aws/anthropic/bedrock-claude-opus-5-5","usage":{"input":2